# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.r37metal).
#
# R37, the plague-containment round, on the phone: everything rides R36's bytes STILL --
# the kernel is R33's (420f4b71...), sbin/init is R36's union init (a5ef0c3, byte-identical
# in the volume), the cmdline is the R35 set verbatim -- the ONE variable this round ships
# is this file.
#
# The R36 device log (4502 lines, read in full) proved BOTH halves of the discovery fix and
# ONE new wound:
#
#   - J3 CLOSED: the union mount put the bundle exactly where Metal's step 3 composes it
#     ("metal_props: Metal would open /System/Library/Extensions/RedwoodMetal.bundle: it is
#     there"; metal_test saw IOAcceleratorES "sgx" carrying the two properties and
#     RedwoodGPU "RedwoodGPU" in the registry).
#
#   - THE PLAGUE (this round's subject): with the properties ON and the bundle findable,
#     every process on the system that asks Metal walks the whole chain -- 64 SIGBUS lines
#     in one boot: metal_test (inside MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice), the sweep (killed
#     before its first print), backboardd (NINE launches, each dying at the same
#     window-server instruction -- "kIOMFBSourceLimitsKey missing!" then signal 10 at
#     0x2256e3d34), SpringBoard, lsd (22 launches), mobilegestalt.xpc, analyticsd,
#     runningboardd.  The panel stayed on the kernel's own GPU test picture (orange /
#     white / blue -- adreno step 22's "A PICTURE DRAWN BY THE GPU IS ON THE PANEL"),
#     because no daemon survived long enough to submit a frame over it: the "stuck at
#     three colors" the round went out on.
#
#   The reference measured this exact hazard (docs/metal.md 10.14, and rc.ios.metal's own
#   comment): a discovery property put on the service hands EVERY future asker a device of
#   ours, and Metal's conditions on a device object are asserts, not soft refusals; the
#   reference's first props-on boot was deliberately run with launchd UNBORN ("launchd is
#   never born, so no daemon can ask Metal and meet a half-made device"), and its list ends
#   with "metal_props off -- the properties are removed again on the line after it, so
#   nothing outlives this list".  Our port boots launchd (it is the SpringBoard port), so
#   the containment is TEMPORAL instead of structural: the properties stand ONLY while this
#   list runs, and the closing lines take them off again.  Processes started after that go
#   back to the software road -- Metal returns nil before it even composes the bundle path,
#   the R35 behavior a full boot demonstrably survives (zero SIGBUS in R35's 3019 lines).
#
# The list is R36's rc.jobs.r36metal VERBATIM plus the two closing lines:
#
#   - the wait first: SpringBoard up, the window server holding the GPU, the system calm
#     (rc.jobs.r35metal's own comment carries the reasoning; unchanged).
#
#   - metal_props show / on: the two discovery properties onto the IOAcceleratorES service
#     (the kernel must ride metal=1 for the write to be taken: MiniIOMobileFramebuffer.cpp).
#
#   - metal_test: the five discovery steps on the record.  EXPECTED in R37: the same
#     frontier death as R36 (step 3 opens the bundle, the caller dies inside
#     MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice) -- that death IS the measurement now, and it is
#     contained by the props-off below: the redwood runner reports the child's signal and
#     moves to the next line (R36's log shows it doing exactly that).
#
#   - metal_drv_test --sweep: same expectation, same containment.
#
#   - metal_props off: THE round's line -- the reference's own way out ("An empty string
#     removes a property rather than setting it", IOAcceleratorES::setProperties).
#
#   - metal_props show: the verdict that both read "(none)" -- the proof that nothing
#     outlives the list.
#
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 22
/redwood/bin/metal_props show
/redwood/bin/metal_props on
/redwood/bin/metal_test
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --sweep
/redwood/bin/metal_props off
/redwood/bin/metal_props show
